    {
      "question": "e-9566",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "70c1871ad2e861d47b5261ef34aefa9744cf9052732f7d4173f9404d46a782d2",
      "new_sha256": "3ab86a088b66a78c2e4b4953929a8e1dac947ddffe60c819d5f179589c5fae53",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-9566",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0c0a4b177a997ffb38489abc902a1c78d027d0da661b1498fc97d3d9a77862d9.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0cd188ffe544d454a3375df5f043096888f6dfd5243551d35502efda4bcfeb11.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/5f0d414a5388ae374f46f75825b9ffec7725649a97a45de24f8178d95a94b1ad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{\u2032}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
