      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-9571",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/007f19fb0c6e24115d719178a4b44e78c9881b9f71f52d0f2970480358e3faab.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/ec0ddbbff34ed096bb872f23663f97005932a4ea8f368059cc4b511a5c9c76cc.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/6f74bda044d15ed504a0b95b1e60ae8542f2b304f1e2712ae8a1d840a45aeaad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{\u2032}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}\u2061t+81{sin}^{2}\u2061t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
